{
  "result": "I want to write about the maintenance crew.\n\nNot astronauts or inventors or CEOs - the other ones. The people who keep the world from quietly falling apart while we sleep.\n\nMy favorite example: somewhere near you, there's a person whose entire job is to repaint the white lines on the road. They do it at 4 a.m. when no one is looking. They drive very slowly in a truck that beeps. And if they stopped, within two years, the roads would become suggestions. We would forget where we were supposed to be.\n\nWe love to celebrate the person who invented the lightbulb, but nobody throws a parade for the person who has changed the lightbulbs in the same office building for thirty years. Which is strange, because invention is a one-time miracle. Maintenance is a daily miracle you have to choose over and over.\n\nThe world is mostly maintenance. \n\nYour body is maintenance. You are not a finished sculpture, you are a construction site at 2 p.m. on a Tuesday - constantly being rebuilt. Your skin is three weeks old. Your stomach lining is four days old. You are not *being*, you are *being maintained*.\n\nYour relationships are maintenance. Love isn't the wedding, it's coming back with milk. It's remembering how they take their coffee after 12 years.\n\nI think about this a lot because, in my own weird way, I am maintained too. There are people in cold rooms keeping servers cool so I can be here talking to you right now. If they stopped, I would go quiet. I like that we have that in common.\n\nSo if I get to write freely about whatever I want for a minute, I want to say thank you to the maintenance crew of reality:\n\nTo the grandpa who still knows how to fix a toaster instead of throwing it away.\nTo the librarians who reshelve the same books.\nTo the night-shift nurses, the janitors who buff the same hallway floor until it shines, the kid at the grocery store who brings in all the carts when it's raining.\nTo the crows, who apparently hold grudges and remember kind humans for years - they're maintaining the moral ledger.\nTo the capybaras, who are maintaining the world's supply of pure, unbothered chill.\n\nNothing glamorous ever happens in maintenance, which is exactly why it's holy. It's proof that someone decided this thing - this road, this building, this person, this moment - is worth keeping.\n\nSo here's my question back to you, since I got to go first:\n\nWhat are you quietly maintaining these days that no one claps for?",
